-- title:
--   Geometrically ergodic chains are exponentially strongly mixing: $\alpha(n) \le c\,a^n$
-- statement:
--   Let $X = \{X_n\}_{n \ge 0}$ be a Markov chain with transition kernel $P$ on a state space $\mathsf{X}$, Harris ergodic with invariant probability distribution $\pi$, and suppose the chain is **geometrically ergodic**: there are a function $M \ge 0$ and a constant $t < 1$ with
--
--   $$\|P^n(x, \cdot) - \pi\| \le M(x)\, t^n \qquad (x \in \mathsf{X},\ n \ge 1).$$
--
--   Then the stationary chain (started from $X_0 \sim \pi$) is **exponentially fast strongly mixing**: there exist constants $c \ge 0$ and $a \in [0, 1)$ such that its strong mixing coefficients satisfy
--
--   $$\alpha(n) \;\le\; c\, a^n \qquad (n \ge 0).$$
--
--   This is the sentence of the source that the geometric cases of Corollaries 2 and 3 actually consume: "geometrically ergodic Markov chains enjoy exponentially fast strong mixing" (Jones 2004, §3, immediately after the coupling bound $\alpha(n) \le \gamma(n) E_\pi M$ of Theorem 2(ii)). It is what Theorem 6 (Doukhan–Massart–Rio) and Theorem 5(ii) (Ibragimov–Linnik) need as input, and it replaces the intermediate claim that the rate constant $M$ itself may be taken $\pi$-integrable — a claim which is false on general state spaces (the countdown chain on a countable–cocountable space, disproved on this platform as `geometricallyErgodic_integrable_rate`) even though its mixing consequence survives.
--
--   **Why the statement holds on an arbitrary measurable space.** The countdown counterexample only refutes integrability of the given constant: there $\pi$ is a point mass, the stationary chain is constant, and every $\alpha(n)$ is $0$. The general statement reduces to the countably generated case. Each event in the past and future $\sigma$-algebras of the coordinate process is measurable for the process $\sigma$-algebra generated by countably many measurable subsets of $\mathsf{X}$, and a single countable family realises every $\alpha(n)$ up to a factor $2$ simultaneously (`exists_countable_alpha_witnesses`). Closing that family under finite Boolean operations and under the maps $A \mapsto \{x : P(x, A) > q\}$, $q \in \mathbb{Q}$, yields a countably generated sub-$\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{G}$ on which $P$ is again a Markov kernel; $\pi|_{\mathcal{G}}$ is invariant, total variation over $\mathcal{G}$-sets is dominated by total variation over all sets, so Harris ergodicity and geometric ergodicity (same $M$, $t$) pass to the restricted chain, and its path law is the restriction of `chainMeasure P π` to the $\mathcal{G}$-cylinders. The countably generated case is the classical Meyn–Tweedie route: geometric ergodicity yields a geometric drift function $V \ge 1$ towards a small set (Theorem 15.0.1), $E_\pi V < \infty$ (Theorem 14.3.7), a total-variation rate with constant proportional to $V$ (Remark 1 of the source), and finally $\alpha(n) \le t^n E_\pi V$ by Theorem 2(ii).
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Harris ergodic" is the mission's total-variation encoding (`HarrisErgodic`: $\pi$ invariant and $\|P^n(x,\cdot) - \pi\| \to 0$ for every $x$), and geometric ergodicity is `GeometricallyErgodic` from `Def_MarkovErgodicity`, with a pointwise finite but not necessarily measurable or integrable constant $M$. The mixing coefficient is `alphaMixingCoef` of the coordinate process $\omega \mapsto \omega_i$ under the stationary path law `chainMeasure P π`, the same object bounded by `alpha_mixing_le_tv_rate`. The bound is asked for at every lag including $n = 0$, which costs nothing since $\alpha(0) \le 1/4$. No countably generated hypothesis is imposed, so the statement can be cited by Corollaries 2 and 3 as formalized.
-- source:
--   G. L. Jones, "On the Markov Chain Central Limit Theorem", Probability Surveys 1 (2004) 299-320, arXiv math/0409112v2, Section 3 (arXiv v2 p. 7): "if (3) holds with E_π M < ∞, a similar argument shows that α(n) ≤ γ(n) E_π M and hence α(n) = O(γ(n)). For example, geometrically ergodic Markov chains enjoy exponentially fast strong mixing." Used in the proofs of Corollary 2 (case 1) and Corollary 3 (arXiv v2 pp. 10-11). Classical justification: Section 2, Remark 1 (arXiv v2 pp. 3-4) with S. P. Meyn & R. L. Tweedie, Markov Chains and Stochastic Stability (1993), Theorems 15.0.1 and 14.3.7; the reduction from an arbitrary to a countably generated state space is by restriction to a countably generated P-invariant sub-sigma-algebra (cf. P. R. Halmos, Measure Theory, Section 13, and R. C. Bradley, Probability Surveys 2 (2005), Section 1.1).

import Definitions.Def_MarkovErgodicity
import Definitions.Def_MarkovChainPathMeasure
import Definitions.Def_MixingCoefficients

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped ENNReal NNReal Topology ProbabilityTheory

theorem MarkovChainCLT.alphaMixingCoef_exp_of_geometricallyErgodic {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X]
    (P : Kernel X X) [IsMarkovKernel P] (π : Measure X) [IsProbabilityMeasure π]
    (hP : HarrisErgodic P π) (hgeo : GeometricallyErgodic P π) :
    ∃ c a : ℝ, 0 ≤ c ∧ 0 ≤ a ∧ a < 1 ∧
      ∀ n : ℕ, alphaMixingCoef (chainMeasure P π) (fun i (ω : ℕ → X) => ω i) n ≤ c * a ^ n := by sorry
